<% content_for :title, "Hinduism | Bipolar Sober" %>

<main class="spirituality-hinduism-page">

  <!-- HERO -->
  <section class="spirituality-hinduism-hero">

    <div class="spirituality-hinduism-hero-content">

      <p class="spirituality-hinduism-eyebrow">
        BIPOLAR SOBER / SPIRITUALITY / HINDUISM
      </p>

      <h1>
        <span>YOU ARE</span>
        <span>MORE THAN</span>
        <span>THIS MOMENT.</span>
      </h1>

      <p class="spirituality-hinduism-intro">
        SELF. DHARMA. KARMA. YOGA.<br>
        ACTION WITHOUT CLINGING TO THE OUTCOME.
      </p>

    </div>

  </section>


  <!-- CONTENT -->
  <section class="spirituality-hinduism-content">


    <!-- 01 -->
    <article class="sh-section sh-section-black">

      <p class="sh-number">01 / THE BIG FUCKING PICTURE</p>

      <h2>
        THERE IS<br>
        MORE TO YOU<br>
        THAN THIS.
      </h2>

      <div class="sh-copy">

        <p>
          Hinduism isn't one single belief system with one book,
          one teacher, or one way of understanding existence.
        </p>

        <p>
          It's a huge family of traditions, philosophies, practices,
          stories, rituals, and schools of thought that developed
          over thousands of years.
        </p>

        <p>
          Different traditions disagree about some pretty major shit —
          including the nature of God, the Self, and ultimate reality.
        </p>

        <p>
          But many ask versions of the same enormous questions:
        </p>

        <div class="sh-stack">
          <span>WHO AM I?</span>
          <span>HOW SHOULD I LIVE?</span>
          <span>WHY DO WE SUFFER?</span>
          <span>WHAT ARE MY ACTIONS CREATING?</span>
          <span>WHAT DOES FREEDOM ACTUALLY MEAN?</span>
        </div>

      </div>

    </article>


    <!-- 02 -->
    <article class="sh-section sh-section-pink">

      <p class="sh-number">02 / THE SELF</p>

      <h2>
        WHO THE<br>
        FUCK<br>
        AM I?
      </h2>

      <div class="sh-copy">

        <p>
          Some Hindu philosophical traditions distinguish between
          the changing parts of experience and a deeper Self,
          often discussed through the idea of <strong>Atman</strong>.
        </p>

        <p>
          Your thoughts change.
          Your emotions change.
          Your circumstances change.
          Your body changes.
          Your roles change.
        </p>

        <p>
          Yet we usually experience some sense of continuity beneath
          all that change.
        </p>

        <div class="sh-callout">
          IF EVERYTHING I'M EXPERIENCING<br>
          IS CHANGING,<br>
          WHAT IS IT THAT'S AWARE OF THE CHANGE?
        </div>

        <p>
          Hindu traditions answer that question differently.
          But the question itself can be powerful.
        </p>

      </div>

    </article>


    <!-- 03 -->
    <article class="sh-section sh-section-yellow">

      <p class="sh-number">03 / DHARMA</p>

      <h2>
        WHAT IS<br>
        MINE TO<br>
        FUCKING DO?
      </h2>

      <div class="sh-copy">

        <p>
          Dharma is a huge concept. Depending on context, it can refer
          to duty, right conduct, moral order, truth, responsibility,
          or a way of living in harmony with a larger order.
        </p>

        <p>
          For everyday reflection, I like the question:
        </p>

        <div class="sh-thought">
          WHAT IS MINE TO DO RIGHT NOW?
        </div>

        <p>
          Not what should everybody else do.
          Not what should have happened yesterday.
          Not how do I control the next six months.
        </p>

        <p>
          What is the next right action that's actually mine?
        </p>

      </div>

    </article>


    <!-- 04 -->
    <article class="sh-section sh-section-purple">

      <p class="sh-number">04 / KARMA</p>

      <h2>
        ACTIONS<br>
        HAVE<br>
        CONSEQUENCES.
      </h2>

      <div class="sh-copy">

        <p>
          Karma is a lot more complicated than
          <strong>"do something bad and the universe punches you back."</strong>
        </p>

        <p>
          Broadly, karma concerns action and its consequences.
          What we intentionally do matters.
        </p>

        <p>
          Our choices shape habits.
          Habits shape character.
          Actions affect other people.
          What we repeatedly practice can influence who we become.
        </p>

        <div class="sh-callout">
          YOU CAN'T CHANGE EVERY ACTION<br>
          THAT BROUGHT YOU HERE.<br>
          YOU CAN CHOOSE THE NEXT ONE.
        </div>

      </div>

    </article>


    <!-- 05 -->
    <article class="sh-section sh-section-black">

      <p class="sh-number">05 / THE BHAGAVAD GITA</p>

      <h2>
        DO THE WORK.<br>
        RELEASE THE<br>
        FUCKING RESULT.
      </h2>

      <div class="sh-copy">

        <p>
          One of the most influential Hindu texts is the
          <em>Bhagavad Gita</em>, a dialogue between the warrior
          Arjuna and Krishna.
        </p>

        <p>
          Arjuna is overwhelmed by a moral crisis and doesn't know
          what the hell he's supposed to do.
        </p>

        <p>
          One of the Gita's major teachings concerns acting according
          to one's duty without becoming consumed by attachment to
          the fruits of action.
        </p>

        <div class="sh-grid">

          <div class="sh-mini-card">
            <span>YOURS</span>
            <h3>THE ACTION.</h3>
            <p>
              Preparation. Effort. Honesty. Practice.
              Showing up. Making the next choice.
            </p>
          </div>

          <div class="sh-mini-card">
            <span>NOT ENTIRELY YOURS</span>
            <h3>THE OUTCOME.</h3>
            <p>
              Other people's reactions. Timing. Luck.
              Circumstances. Whether life follows your fucking plan.
            </p>
          </div>

        </div>

      </div>

    </article>


    <!-- 06 -->
    <article class="sh-section sh-section-pink">

      <p class="sh-number">06 / YOGA</p>

      <h2>
        IT'S MORE<br>
        THAN FUCKING<br>
        STRETCHING.
      </h2>

      <div class="sh-copy">

        <p>
          In modern Western culture, "yoga" often means physical poses.
        </p>

        <p>
          Hindu traditions use the word much more broadly for spiritual
          disciplines or paths aimed at transformation and liberation.
        </p>

        <div class="sh-grid">

          <div class="sh-mini-card">
            <span>01</span>
            <h3>KARMA YOGA</h3>
            <p>
              The path of action and service without clinging
              to personal reward.
            </p>
          </div>

          <div class="sh-mini-card">
            <span>02</span>
            <h3>BHAKTI YOGA</h3>
            <p>
              The path of devotion, love, and relationship
              with the divine.
            </p>
          </div>

          <div class="sh-mini-card">
            <span>03</span>
            <h3>JNANA YOGA</h3>
            <p>
              The path of knowledge, inquiry, and examining
              the nature of Self and reality.
            </p>
          </div>

          <div class="sh-mini-card">
            <span>04</span>
            <h3>RAJA YOGA</h3>
            <p>
              A contemplative path associated with meditation,
              discipline, and mastery of the mind.
            </p>
          </div>

        </div>

      </div>

    </article>


    <!-- 07 -->
    <article class="sh-section sh-section-yellow">

      <p class="sh-number">07 / THE GUNAS</p>

      <h2>
        YOUR INNER<br>
        WEATHER<br>
        CHANGES.
      </h2>

      <div class="sh-copy">

        <p>
          Some Hindu philosophies describe nature through three
          qualities called the <strong>gunas</strong>.
        </p>

        <div class="sh-grid">

          <div class="sh-mini-card">
            <span>SATTVA</span>
            <h3>CLARITY.</h3>
            <p>
              Harmony, balance, illumination, calm, and understanding.
            </p>
          </div>

          <div class="sh-mini-card">
            <span>RAJAS</span>
            <h3>ACTIVITY.</h3>
            <p>
              Movement, passion, desire, restlessness, and striving.
            </p>
          </div>

          <div class="sh-mini-card">
            <span>TAMAS</span>
            <h3>INERTIA.</h3>
            <p>
              Heaviness, darkness, resistance, dullness, and inactivity.
            </p>
          </div>

        </div>

        <p>
          These are philosophical concepts about qualities of nature —
          <strong>not psychiatric diagnoses.</strong>
        </p>

        <p>
          Mania isn't simply rajas.
          Depression isn't simply tamas.
          Mental illness shouldn't be reduced to spiritual terminology.
        </p>

        <p>
          But the gunas can offer another vocabulary for noticing
          the qualities present in a moment without deciding
          that the moment is your entire identity.
        </p>

      </div>

    </article>


    <!-- 08 -->
    <article class="sh-section sh-section-purple">

      <p class="sh-number">08 / ATTACHMENT</p>

      <h2>
        DO THE WORK.<br>
        LOOSEN YOUR<br>
        FUCKING GRIP.
      </h2>

      <div class="sh-copy">

        <p>
          Hindu traditions frequently wrestle with attachment:
          our tendency to cling to identities, desires, possessions,
          outcomes, and the belief that happiness depends on reality
          obeying us.
        </p>

        <p>
          Detachment doesn't have to mean not caring.
        </p>

        <p>
          It can mean acting wholeheartedly while accepting that
          you do not own the outcome.
        </p>

        <div class="sh-stack">
          <span>I CAN CARE WITHOUT CONTROLLING.</span>
          <span>I CAN TRY WITHOUT GUARANTEEING.</span>
          <span>I CAN LOVE WITHOUT OWNING.</span>
          <span>I CAN ACT WITHOUT KNOWING EXACTLY WHAT HAPPENS NEXT.</span>
        </div>

      </div>

    </article>


    <!-- 09 -->
    <article class="sh-section sh-section-black">

      <p class="sh-number">09 / PRACTICE</p>

      <h2>
        OKAY.<br>
        HOW DO I<br>
        USE THIS?
      </h2>

      <div class="sh-copy">

        <p>
          You don't need to understand thousands of years of Hindu
          philosophy before one idea can make you think differently.
        </p>

        <div class="sh-practice-grid">

          <div>
            <span>01</span>
            <p>Ask: What is actually mine to do right now?</p>
          </div>

          <div>
            <span>02</span>
            <p>Separate your effort from your desired outcome.</p>
          </div>

          <div>
            <span>03</span>
            <p>Notice what you're clinging to or trying to control.</p>
          </div>

          <div>
            <span>04</span>
            <p>Observe your thoughts and emotions without making them your entire identity.</p>
          </div>

          <div>
            <span>05</span>
            <p>Choose an action consistent with your values and responsibilities.</p>
          </div>

          <div>
            <span>06</span>
            <p>Let the next moment arrive before trying to control the next hundred.</p>
          </div>

        </div>

      </div>

    </article>


    <!-- ENDING -->
    <article class="sh-ending">

      <p>
        YOU DON'T CONTROL EVERY OUTCOME.<br>
        YOU DO PARTICIPATE IN WHAT COMES NEXT.
      </p>

      <h2>
        DO YOUR PART.<br>
        RELEASE<br>
        THE REST.
      </h2>

      <p class="sh-ending-copy">
        One action. One choice. One moment at a time.
      </p>

      <%= link_to "← BACK TO SPIRITUALITY",
          spirituality_path,
          class: "sh-back-link" %>

    </article>

  </section>

</main>